import { CorrectionCopy } from "../../correction-model"

pub fn copy -> CorrectionCopy = CorrectionCopy {
  summary: "NonEmptyList names an ordered collection with at least one value. Reading its first element does not need a missing-value result.",
  taskTitle: "Promise that a parameter has a first score",
  purpose: "Use NonEmptyList when a calculation requires a first element. NonEmptyList<Int> names an ordered collection of at least one integer. An ordinary List<Int> allows an empty input, so its type alone cannot promise that a first score exists.",
  setup: "import * as nonempty from \"std/non-empty-list\" makes this type and its operations available under the name nonempty. let scores: nonempty.NonEmptyList<Int> = ... names a value scores and checks it against the type after the colon. Int means an integer, and <Int> chooses the element type. `[20, 30] is a List literal, including the backtick before [. Without that backtick, [20, 30] is an Array, a different kind of sequence.",
  codeTitle: "Read a guaranteed first score and check a possibly empty input",
  outputTitle: "Seseragi output",
  after: [
    "nonempty.cons 10 `[20, 30] takes the first score 10 and the remaining List to construct a NonEmptyList containing 10, 20, 30. Spaces separate the call's arguments. head scores returns the first Int directly, so the first output is 10. The type guarantees that this element exists; the result of head needs no missing-value branch.",
    "tail returns an ordinary List: tail scores excludes the first element, producing `[20, 30] on the second line. singleton 0 constructs a NonEmptyList containing only zero. Its tail is the empty List `[], shown on the third line. Having a first element does not promise a second one. Parentheses group the result of an inner call as one argument to another call.",
    "fn firstOrNone values: List<Int> -> String = ... declares a function accepting an ordinary List and returning text. The name follows fn; values names its argument and the colon introduces the argument's type. -> String gives the return type, and the expression after = supplies that value. values is whichever list the caller supplies.",
    "fromList values checks the input and returns Just scores when a first element exists, or Nothing when the input is empty. Just wraps the constructed NonEmptyList; Nothing represents its absence. The type containing these two possibilities is named Maybe. match selects the branch that fits. Just scores -> show (nonempty.head scores) names the checked list scores and turns its head into text. Nothing -> \"no scores\" returns the empty-input label. Both branches return text.",
    "firstOrNone `[0, 20] successfully checks the List and prints its first score, 0. Zero is a valid score, distinct from absence. The following firstOrNone `[] takes the Nothing branch and prints no scores."
  ],
  comparisonTitle: "Give TypeScript a required first element too",
  comparisonBefore: "TypeScript readonly [number, ...number[]] means one required number followed by zero or more numbers. [...] specifies types by position, and ...number[] describes the remaining elements. The example names this type NonEmptyScores. By contrast, readonly number[] does not specify a length and permits an empty array. readonly prevents changing elements through that type.",
  comparisonAfter: "The TypeScript conversion function uses const [first, ...rest] = values to separate the first element from the rest. It returns undefined when first === undefined and constructs [first, ...rest] only when a first value exists. ...rest places the remaining elements at that position. In fromArray's return type NonEmptyScores | undefined, | permits either side. scores[0] reads the guaranteed first element; slice(1) creates a new array without that element. The function returns undefined when there is no first element, and the caller selects no scores. Without an unchecked type assertion, it handles the same first score 10, remainder 20 and 30, empty remainder, first score 0, and empty input. JSON array output omits the List backtick and spaces. This TypeScript type models the required-first-element condition, not Seseragi's List representation. readonly itself neither freezes objects at runtime nor validates data arriving from elsewhere.",
  detailTitle: "Establish the guarantee and know when it is lost",
  details: [
    "cons and singleton require a first element when constructing a NonEmptyList. fromList checks an ordinary List and returns a NonEmptyList only on success. tail returns a List, so its result may be empty. reduce1 can use the guaranteed head as the initial value for combining elements. Read each operation's return type to see whether it preserves the guarantee.",
    "After let emptyScores: List<Int> = `[], assigning let scores: nonempty.NonEmptyList<Int> = emptyScores produces SES-T0101. An annotation cannot convert an ordinary List to NonEmptyList. Even when a particular List value happens to be nonempty, its List<Int> type does not promise a first element. Use singleton or cons when you already have a first value; otherwise handle both results of fromList.",
    "fn first values: List<Int> -> Int = nonempty.head values also produces SES-T0101: the parameter allows an empty List, but head requires NonEmptyList. If the function should accept only guaranteed nonempty values, make its parameter NonEmptyList<Int> and construct such a value before calling it. If it should accept empty input too, check fromList as firstOrNone does here.",
    "The _ in the exact declaration NonEmptyList<_> marks the element type you choose. The label opaque-type means the internal representation is private. This type guarantees at least one element, not a particular upper bound. Int and TypeScript number are not identical types either; this comparison uses only the small integers shown."
  ]
}
